; apps/scribe/welcome.wtx - Scribe's welcome page (SPEC.md 95). The Makefile
; builds it twice, as SCWELCOM.RTF (tools/os88rtf.py) and SCWELCOM.DOC
; (tools/os88doc.py), so the text must be true of both files: it names
; neither as "this file's" format. It was a copy of apps/word/welcome.wtx
; until the manuals pass, which put "This document is a native Word .DOC
; file" in an RTF and "Welcome to Microsoft Word" in front of Scribe.
; One paragraph per line; leading '.'-directives set the paragraph format,
; {b}..{/b}-style spans set character formats, a literal tab is a tab.
.c {d}WELCOME TO SCRIBE{/d}
.c The os8088 word processor

Scribe is a word processor forked from os8088's Microsoft Word 1.1a: the same menus, ribbon, ruler, dialogs and keys, plus one thing Word does not have - pictures in the document.

.open {b}Pictures{/b}
Insert Picture puts a .BMP, .PCX or .PIX picture into the text at the caret, in colour or in black and white. One document holds up to eight.

.open {b}Two file formats{/b}
A new document saves as an RTF file (DOCUMENT.RTF) unless you give it a .DOC name with Save As. RTF keeps a picture's colours. A Word .DOC file can only hold black-and-white pictures, so saving as .DOC turns each one black and white.
This page is on the disk twice, as SCWELCOM.RTF and as SCWELCOM.DOC. Scribe takes no file type for itself, so double-clicking a document never starts it: open documents with File Open.

.open {b}What works{/b}
Type anywhere; select with the mouse, or press F8 and extend with the arrow keys. Character formats live on the ribbon, in Format Character, and on the Ctrl keys: {b}bold{/b} (Ctrl-B), {i}italic{/i}, {b}{i}both together{/b}{/i}, {u}underline{/u} (Ctrl-U), {w}word underline{/w} (Ctrl-W), {d}double underline{/d} (Ctrl-D) and {k}small caps{/k} (Ctrl-K). Paragraph formats come from the ruler, the Ctrl keys, or Format Paragraph.
.li 5 .fi -5 This paragraph wears a hanging indent, set with Ctrl-T; the ruler's markers show it, and dragging them moves it.
.c This paragraph is centered (Ctrl-C).
.r This one is flush right (Ctrl-R).
.sp2 .open This one is double spaced (Ctrl-2) and open (Ctrl-O).

.open {b}A few keys{/b}
Ctrl-F	Search
F4 / Shift-F4	Repeat the search down / up
F5	Go To a page
Ins	Overtype (the status bar's OVR lamp)
F8	Extend the selection (EXT)
Shift-F12	Save
F12	Save As
Alt-BkSp	Undo
Alt-F4	Exit

Save your own documents into the DOCS folder with Save As; File Open reads plain text files too.
